the 2020 race for the white house  is more unpredictable than ever a  campaign already upended  by the coronavirus pandemic dozens of  ballots were thrown into a dumpster  new concern that election day could  stretch well beyond november 3rd  the economist is analyzing the latest  data to cut through the noise and  predict  the results you submitted your questions  to eliot morris  our data journalist and election guru  here are his answers  [Music]  in 2016 hillary clinton was the favorite  by a large margin  why should we trust the data this time  that is the million dollar question  right uh so polls this time  should be better but that's no guarantee  and there are a few reasons  why that's the case the first is the  technical methodological explanation  and that is that the reason that some  polls were wrong especially in the  midwest  in states like michigan and wisconsin uh  was because they  they missed uh the crucial block of  non-college-wide voters in the  electorate that support  donald trump by a few percentage points  and the reason that's the case is  because those voters are less likely to  take  public opinion polls than voters with a  college degree who are more likely to  support  democrats this year it seems like most  pollsters are making those adjustments  correctly and that the polls should not  underestimate support for trump in the  same way this year  that they did last time around there's  also  another consideration that might make  polls better and that's that there  are fewer undecided voters in the  electorate this time around  so in 2016 there was upwards of 15  percent  of voters who said they were unlikely to  vote or they didn't know who  they were going to vote for or they were  going to vote for a third-party  candidate  and this year that number in total is  really closer to five percent  and that makes us more confident that  joe biden's lead in the polls is robust  and likely to last  how many unknowns do you suspect could  make election predictions inaccurate  there are a few things that could make  our predictions wrong and the first  thing is obviously having to do with the  polls  if they're talking to the wrong people  or there's a late shift  that happens after the last polls are  done sampling  sampling voters then they could go awry  but this year there are also some  particular problems  with the pandemic so pollsters rely on  asking people  if they'll actually turn out to vote and  if  one party is more likely to overestimate  their tendency to actually vote  then the polls would overestimate their  support so you can imagine a pandemic  in a year where donald trump is telling  most of his voters to vote in  person that republicans could be more  exposed to  uh telling pollsters that they're more  likely to vote than they really are  and that could make uh polls biased  against them now that's just one story  that's just conjecture  but it's one potential source of error  what does voter turnout look like in the  swing states  so voter turnout looks to be up pretty  much everywhere that includes in swing  states  and in other states in some states early  voting already accounts for 20  or more of total turnout in 2016  which is quite astonishing and that has  downstream electoral consequences as  well so  we know from past research at the  economist that when turnout  is higher that tends to benefit  democrats and that's mainly in diverse  states so  the sun built states that look  competitive this year like arizona  texas georgia and north carolina and to  some extent florida which is  always competitive really joe biden has  an upside  and that more voters are turning out  this time than would have in 2016.  do you incorporate early voting into  your forecasting model  our model doesn't use early voting data  and most  forecasting models don't and there are a  few reasons  why the first is that it's hard to get  data from each state  on who exactly is casting early ballots  so some states might tell you that there  are democrats and republicans voting and  this is how many ballots are coming from  them  but most states don't even if a state  does tell you that there are democrats  or republicans voting  there's a difference between when  somebody registers as a democrat or  republican with the government  and they also by the way have the  ability to register with no party  in some states and how they actually  vote a recent poll in pennsylvania  for example found uh that about  70 of registered republicans were  actually voting for donald trump  uh which leaves about 30 percent who  aren't so it's just hard to guess  at which partisans are going to vote for  who the real  forecasting information comes from  the state of the economy and the  president's approval rating  so we know historically when a president  is  has has a low popularity or when the  economy is  shrinking that they tend to do poorly in  the election  does your model consider high rejection  rates of ballots  there are a few ways that high rejection  rates of ballots  could influence our predictions uh  now historically uh there is some  disconnect  uh between who people say they're going  to vote for and the actual  count of ballots in a state that can  explain some  pollster error of course and rejection  rates  is one of them so we know in most states  that  uh younger and african-american voters  have their ballots rejected at higher  rates  and historically that that the error  caused by this dynamic is captured  in our election model but if there is a  spike  this year in how likely rejections are  for younger or african-american or  lower-income voters  that could cause further error in the  model  and we know from from separate modeling  that that could  very well be true this year that joe  biden  could face an extra one or two  percentage point drop  in severe states for rejection rates uh  just based  on on slow delivery on young people  not having their signatures match up  with the state file and the sort of  numerous other reasons for rejections  can in ballots be discarded as easily as  some fear  [Music]  so the the sort of conspiracy theories  about  people just discarding ballots or  putting them in the trash  are quite overblown those are really  rare occasions that  don't impact a lot of votes but there  are  real concerns about the ways that the  laws around absentee ballots are  structured  in america they can have  disproportionate effects like we've  talked about for  younger and non-white americans  who move around a lot so they don't have  an address for election workers to  verify  or who don't have driver's licenses so  they don't have signatures  already in the state's voter system that  that election workers use to verify  absentee ballots  uh or in lower income areas we know the  postal service  uh works more slowly than it does in  other areas of the country for whatever  reason  uh which could make those ballots late  so so i'm less worried about people  intentionally discarding ballots  and reformers are more worried about the  real structural problems with the system  how long can we expect to wait for the  results  so we should have a really good idea of  what's going to happen  by around 9 or 10 p.m eastern time and  that's because that's when florida  should have about 90 or 95 of its  ballots casted at least  if patterns from 2016 uh hold true  and the reason that florida is so  important  is because it tells us a lot about how  other states are going to vote  so the way our election model works is  by  arranging states from left to right  based off of their projected vote  share essentially and we know if florida  is going blue then most of the states  to the left of florida like the  midwestern states or  maybe even arizona this year are also  likely to vote for joe biden so  essentially if he wins florida and we  should know pretty early if he does  then he's on track to win the election  our model would give him close to  a 95 chance you know a pretty sure thing  uh barring unforeseen consequences at  that point we would be able to predict  the result  could one candidate appear to be winning  on election night  but lose once all ballots have been  counted  there's been a lot of concern this year  about this so-called red mirage or blue  wave  effect where absentee ballots which can  sometimes take a while to count  are more democratic leaning than other  ballots so the thinking goes this year  that because there are going to be so  many absentee ballots and because we're  in a pandemic  and we might have fewer poll workers  that they'll take longer to count  and that's probably true however  the the chance for the electoral college  to flip over time but after  a lot of ballots are counted is  relatively low  the economist looked at this earlier  this year and found that  once 80 percent or so of both absentee  and election day ballots are counted  then the election would line up with the  result  how likely are americans to not accept  the election result  and what would be the impact of this  yeah that's a really important question  this year when we have  politicians questioning the results  already  it's pretty likely that citizens would  do the same  and we have polls that show from yougov  and from a partnership between ucla and  this  research organization called the  democracy fund that people are pretty  likely to endorse  violence or unrest if their side loses  especially in the case where they view  that loss  as undemocratic so republicans in the  case  of widespread illegal voting which by  the way  we haven't ever seen evidence before of  our likelier to endorse violence against  opponents or again widespread unrest  and democrats are likely to do the same  if there's a mismatch between the  electoral college and the popular vote  like there was last time around  but when we talk about tolerance for  violence that's also a lot different  than actually going out and you know  hurting your opponents i think this is  less  of a concern if either candidate wins by  a large amount  where you know if joe biden has the  eight or nine point margin that our  forecast  is uh projecting today then it's harder  to call the election and the dow  hi i'm elliot morris a member of the  economists  data team with election day less than  four weeks away  you can join my colleagues from our u.s  team for a live digital event we're  hosting  especially for economist readers and  viewers  we'll be discussing the economic impact  of the election  and answering any questions you may have  about the remainder  of the campaign this event is completely  free  to register for it just click on the  link thanks for watching

